  }
  footer { color: var(--muted); font-size: .84rem; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    * { transition: none !important; animation: none !important; }
  }
</style>

<div class="wrap">
  <header>
    <div class="eyebrow">SuperSTEM &middot; Daresbury</div>
    <h1>Instrument Survey</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      One read-only script, three runs, three JSON files to send back. It answers
      questions about your instruments that we are currently guessing at &mdash; and
      guessing wrong costs weeks of the wrong adapter.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="get">
    <div>
      <h3>Get the script</h3>
      <div class="filemeta" style="margin-top:.45rem">
        <span><code>superstem_survey.py</code></span>
        <span>__KIB__&nbsp;KB</span>
        <span>__LINES__ lines</span>
        <span>rev __REV__</span>
        <span>no dependencies</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="primary" id="save" href="__GITHUB_RAW_URL__" download="superstem_survey.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download script</a>
      <button class="secondary" id="copy">Copy to clipboard</button>
    </div>
    <div id="status"></div>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted)">
      <strong>Download script</strong> links straight to
      <code>scripts/superstem_survey.py</code> on GitHub &mdash; it works in any
      browser, whether or not you are signed into Claude. If that machine has no
      route to GitHub, use <strong>Copy to clipboard</strong> instead, or open
      &ldquo;Read the script first&rdquo; below, select all, and paste into a new
      <code>.py</code> file.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>Before anything else</h2>
    <p>
      Check the interpreter. The script needs <strong>Python 3.7 or newer</strong> &mdash;
      that floor is set by Gatan Microscopy Suite, which embeds 3.7 from version 3.4 and is the oldest
      Python plausibly sitting near this hardware. On anything older the file will not
      parse, and you get a <code>SyntaxError</code> rather than a useful message.
    </p>
    <pre class="cmd">python -c "import sys; print(sys.version)"</pre>
    <p>Then run the preflight. It touches no hardware and opens no socket:</p>
    <pre class="cmd">python superstem_survey.py --check</pre>
    <p>
      It prints which interpreter it is in and which sections that interpreter could
      answer. If it disagrees with what you expect, <strong>the interpreter is the thing
      to change, not the machine.</strong> (If you copy-pasted the script instead of
      downloading it, name the file whatever you like &mdash; Python runs it either way.)
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <span class="lede">Which Python matters more than which computer</span>
      <p>
        Two of the three sections can only see what is importable from the interpreter
        they run in, and running from the wrong one gives a confident wrong answer rather
        than an error. A missing module from a Python the vendor software never installed
        into is not evidence of absence. If a machine has several Pythons, run it once
        per interpreter with a different <code>--out</code> and send all the files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it will not do</h2>
    <p>
      The script's safety is structural, not a matter of care. Precisely what that means
      for each instrument:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Nion.</strong> Reads a component registry that something else populated,
        and reads named controls. Never sets a control, never blanks or unblanks, never
        moves the stage, never starts a scan &mdash; and never loads a device plug-in.
        If the registry is empty it says so and asks you to run it from Swift's own
        Python console, rather than populating the registry itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>DECTRIS.</strong> HTTP <code>GET</code> only. Never writes a
        configuration, never arms, triggers, disarms or aborts. Safe to run while
        somebody else is using the detector &mdash; and the state it reports will tell
        you if somebody is.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hitachi.</strong> Resolves module <em>specifications</em>, which locates
        a module without executing it, and lists directories. Never imports a vendor
        control module, because importing one may open a connection to the column.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gatan.</strong> Locates the <code>DigitalMicrograph</code> module without
        importing it, and notices when it is <em>already loaded</em> &mdash; which is how
        it knows it is inside Gatan Microscopy Suite's own Python window. Executes no DM
        script and reads no image.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No section reads or transmits acquired data. The report contains instrument
      capability, versions and file paths &mdash; no images, no spectra, no credentials.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The runs</h2>
    <p>
      Three instruments and four runs. They are independent &mdash; do them in whatever
      order suits the schedule. The Nion run is wanted on <em>every</em> Nion column,
      because it answers a different question on each.
    </p>

    <div class="run">
      <div class="runhead"><span class="runno">RUN 1</span><h2>SuperSTEM 2 &mdash; Nion UltraSTEM 100</h2></div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Where</dt><dd>The instrument control computer</dd>
        <dt>Interpreter</dt><dd><strong>Nion Swift's own Python console</strong>, while Swift is running</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre class="cmd">python superstem_survey.py --nion --out superstem2.json</pre>
      <p>
        <strong>The highest-value run of the set.</strong> It asks whether the STEM
        controller exposes an <code>eels_camera</code> and publishes an energy-offset
        control &mdash; under either of two names, <code>ZLPoffset</code> (the simulator's)
        or <code>EELS_MagneticShift_Offset</code> (Nion's own). If it does both, the UHV
        Enfina is already reachable through Nion, the spectrometer is already drivable,
        and no Gatan-side code is needed here at all. If it does neither, an adapter that
        has to live inside Gatan Microscopy Suite comes back onto the plan &mdash; and
        that plan has a hole in it: SuperSTEM 2 runs DigitalMicrograph 2.x, which has no
        Python, so nothing of ours can run inside DM there. Run 4's <code>--gatan</code>
        from any Python on the machine still records the DM installation.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also lists the installed Swift plug-ins (by file, never loaded), every
        registered camera's own account of itself &mdash; sensor shape, binning, dark and
        gain support, calibration controls &mdash; the scan unit's channels, each
        hardware source's saved profiles, and whether the scan and cameras offer Nion's
        synchronised-acquisition methods, which is whether a spectrum image can be taken
        through Nion's stack. All of it is property reads and <code>hasattr</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Safe to run during a session. A system Python on the same computer will usually
        report an empty registry &mdash; that is a fact about that interpreter, not about
        your instrument.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="run">
      <div class="runhead"><span class="runno">RUN 2</span><h2>HERMES &mdash; the column, then the DECTRIS control unit</h2></div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Where, first</dt><dd>The control computer, <strong>from Nion Swift's own Python console</strong></dd>
      </dl>
      <pre class="cmd">python superstem_survey.py --nion --out superstem3.json</pre>
      <p>
        The mirror image of Run 1's question: <strong>does Nion's stack <em>also</em>
        register an EELS camera on this column?</strong> The ELA is served by its own
        process in our configuration; if Nion registers it too, that is two drivers on
        one detector and the configuration has to choose. The same run records whether
        the IRIS spectrometer's dispersion and offset are published as column controls,
        which is where the ELA's frames would get an energy axis from.
      </p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Where, second</dt><dd>Any machine on the control network, a laptop included</dd>
        <dt>Interpreter</dt><dd>Any Python 3.7+</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre class="cmd">python superstem_survey.py --dectris 192.168.1.10 --out hermes.json</pre>
      <p>
        Use the address of the <strong>control unit</strong>, not the detector head
        &mdash; SIMPLON is served by the DCU on port 80. The address above is an example.
      </p>
      <p>
        This settles which SIMPLON version answers, which configuration keys an ELA
        actually publishes, the trigger mode it is left in, and whether the
        <code>monitor</code> and <code>stream</code> subsystems are enabled. Our adapter
        was written against published documentation and open-source clients; several of
        its assumptions are guesses that one <code>GET</code> each can confirm or kill.
        Safe to run mid-experiment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="run">
      <div class="runhead"><span class="runno">RUN 3</span><h2>SuperSTEM 4 &mdash; Hitachi SU9000II</h2></div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Where</dt><dd>The SU9000II control computer</dd>
        <dt>Interpreter</dt><dd>Whichever Python the vendor software installed into &mdash; if unsure, all of them</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre class="cmd">python superstem_survey.py --hitachi --out superstem4.json</pre>
      <p>
        This looks for three undocumented external-control modules &mdash;
        <code>MfExtCont</code>, <code>MfKeyMouse</code>, <code>MfCommon</code> &mdash;
        evidenced on an SU7000 and unknown on an SU9000II, and for install folders named
        for Hitachi, EM Flow Creator and ElementView. Whether the modules are present is
        the single fact deciding between an adapter we can write in a couple of weeks and
        a conversation with Hitachi.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing is imported, so nothing connects to the column.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="run">
      <div class="runhead"><span class="runno">RUN 4</span><h2>SuperSTEM 1 &mdash; find out what drives it</h2></div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Where</dt><dd>The instrument control computer</dd>
        <dt>Interpreter</dt><dd>Any Python 3.7+ on the machine &mdash; then Swift's console, if Swift is there</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre class="cmd">python superstem_survey.py --check
python superstem_survey.py --all --out superstem1.json</pre>
      <p>
        We know one thing about SuperSTEM 1 first-hand: its spectrometer reads out
        1340&times;100. What drives the column and what drives the Enfina are both
        unknown, so this run is a diagnosis. <code>--all</code> runs the Nion, Gatan and
        Hitachi sections and reports which of them found anything. If
        <code>--check</code> says <code>nion: yes</code>, run the Nion section again from
        Swift's own console as in Run 1.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>SuperSTEM 1 runs DigitalMicrograph 1.x and SuperSTEM 2 runs 2.x, and
        neither has Python built in</strong> &mdash; that arrived with GMS 3.4. So there
        is no DM Python window to run anything from on either machine. What
        <code>--gatan</code> still records from any Python is where DM is installed and
        any GMS environment variables. The facts that matter most on SuperSTEM 1 are for
        a person: which program drives the column, which drives the Enfina, and the DM
        version on the About box. They are in the questions below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Questions only you can answer</h2>
    <p>
      The script reads what the software publishes. These are the things it cannot, and
      they matter as much: they are what decides whether a window we build is one you
      would recognise. A few minutes per instrument, a sentence each.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Which software, today.</strong> On each column, what do you take a STEM
        image, an EELS spectrum and a spectrum image in &mdash; Nion Swift,
        DigitalMicrograph, something site-specific? Which Swift version? Is Swift's own
        spectrum-imaging panel used, and does it work? On SuperSTEM 1 and 2, which
        DigitalMicrograph version exactly (the About box), and on SuperSTEM 1, what
        drives the column at all?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The spectrometer.</strong> How do you set the dispersion (eV per channel),
        and where? Which dispersions and energy ranges do you actually use? How do you
        move the zero-loss peak &mdash; which control, in which software? Is there a
        drift-tube or slit setting you adjust during a session?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dark and gain.</strong> How do you take a dark reference, and how often?
        Is gain normalisation used, and where does the reference come from?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A typical spectrum image.</strong> Grid size, per-position exposure,
        dispersion, which images you keep alongside (HAADF, MAADF), whether drift
        correction is on and what does it, and roughly how long a pass takes. Do you draw
        the region on a live image or type the size?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A typical STEM image.</strong> Dwell and size for looking, and for
        keeping. Scan rotation routinely? Sub-regions?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>SuperSTEM 4 specifically.</strong> Who makes the EELS spectrometer and
        the diffraction camera, and which software drives each? Is
        <strong>EM Flow Creator</strong> installed and licensed? Is
        <strong>ElementView</strong> there, and does it have any scripting, macro, batch
        or watch-folder option?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>HERMES, for a later hardware slot.</strong> Is the scan unit's per-pixel
        trigger output wired to the ELA's external trigger input, and which trigger mode
        is the ELA in when Swift takes a spectrum image? And, when somebody is watching:
        does one software <code>trigger</code> in <code>ints</code> mode produce one image
        or start the whole series? That last one needs the detector armed, which is
        exactly what this script refuses to do.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Sending results back</h2>
    <p>
      One JSON file per run. They are small, they are text, and they contain no acquired
      data &mdash; reading one through before sending takes a moment and is worth doing
      if anything in it looks sensitive.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If a run fails, send the file anyway.</strong> Every probe is wrapped so a
      failure is recorded as data rather than stopping the run. A report full of errors
      still says which questions the machine could not answer and why, which is itself an
      answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <details>
    <summary>Read the script first (__LINES__ lines)</summary>
    <pre id="sourceview"></pre>
  </details>

  <footer>
    Revision <code>__REV__</code> of <code>scripts/superstem_survey.py</code> in
    <code>miainwoodpecker</code>. The download and the source above are the same bytes.
  </footer>
</div>

<pre id="src" hidden>__SCRIPT_SOURCE__</pre>

<script>
  (function () {
    var src = document.getElementById("src").textContent;
    document.getElementById("sourceview").textContent = src;

    var copyBtn = document.getElementById("copy");
    var status = document.getElementById("status");

    function say(message, kind) {
      status.textContent = message;
      status.className = kind || "";
    }

    copyBtn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      function fallback() {
        var area = document.createElement("textarea");
        area.value = src;
        area.setAttribute("readonly", "");
        area.style.position = "fixed";
        area.style.opacity = "0";
        document.body.appendChild(area);
        area.select();
        var ok = false;
        try {
          ok = document.execCommand("copy");
        } catch (e) {
          ok = false;
        }
        document.body.removeChild(area);
        if (ok) {
          say("Copied. Paste into an empty file and save it as superstem_survey.py", "ok");
        } else {
          say("Could not copy automatically \u2014 open \u201cRead the script first\u201d below, select all, and copy.", "warn");
        }
      }
      if (navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText) {
        navigator.clipboard.writeText(src).then(function () {
          say("Copied. Paste into an empty file and save it as superstem_survey.py", "ok");
        }, fallback);
      } else {
        fallback();
      }
    });
  })();
</script>
